/**
 * TestPlan – the doc testing tool's script for this page. Renders nothing.
 *
 * One block per page, at the end of the content. A plan is ONE NUMBERED LIST and nothing else: a
 * single linear script that walks every testable route the page documents, in order, reverting state
 * between them. Ordering between procedures, and interference between documented alternatives, are
 * handled by the script rather than guessed at by the tool.
 *
 * A plan is NOT graded against the prose. It is the same flow written for a bot: it names the same
 * controls, and spells out the mechanical steps a human is never told (dismissing an obvious confirm
 * dialog, collapsing a panel that covers a form). A failing step means something on the page is
 * wrong, and the matching doc step is easy to find by hand.
 *
 * There are no sections. Isolation, Setup, Inputs and Notes were headings once; each was a step
 * wearing a hat, and hiding them made real work invisible to the run and to coverage. Every line is
 * a numbered step, so every line is countable. Text that is not numbered is commentary and ignored.
 *
 * A route the tool can't drive gets a `skip` step saying why. Absence of the whole block means
 * nobody has written the plan yet. That distinction is what makes plan coverage countable.
 *
 * A plan is parsed as MDX like the rest of the page, so an angle-bracket placeholder in a step —
 * <database-name> in a documented connection string, say — reads as an unclosed JSX tag and FAILS THE
 * BUILD. Wrap a statement in backticks to make it inline code; the parser strips them back off.
 *
 * Steps use a fixed verb set, so execution stays deterministic and no LLM reads a plan at run time.
 * A line matching no verb is REPORTED, never skipped. The whole set:
 *
 *   Where the run happens
 *     fork the service              run what follows on a throwaway copy; without this the run uses
 *                                   the standing service and refuses every destructive step
 *     select the service            navigate to the service under test
 *     go to /operations             reach a path directly — positioning and resetting state, never a
 *                                   substitute for a documented click
 *
 *   Files
 *     download https://…            fetch what the page tells the reader to download (archives are
 *                                   unzipped; the folder is psql's cwd and the source for `upload`)
 *     upload sample.csv             put a fetched file into a file input
 *
 *   The interface
 *     click `Label`                 a labelled control
 *     click `A` > `B`               a path through menus or nav, one click per hop
 *     click `X` in `Group`          one option out of several identically-labelled ones
 *     toggle `X` on                 a switch; `on`/`off` optional, add "for `row`" to pick a row
 *     type `value` into `Field`     literal text
 *     type $INVITE_EMAIL into `Email`
 *                                   a value from DOCTEST_INPUT_INVITE_EMAIL — never write a real
 *                                   address, CIDR or credential in a plan
 *     use the row for $OWNER_EMAIL  act on the table row holding a value this run owns
 *     choose an unselected option   where the docs leave the choice open ("choose your strategy")
 *
 *   Data
 *     run SQL: SELECT …             inline; multi-line as indented lines under a trailing ":"
 *     run command: `psql -d "…"`    a shell command; documented placeholders are resolved from the
 *                                   environment at run time
 *     exit psql                     end the psql session, so later SQL goes back through the Console
 *
 *   Assertions
 *     expect rows: <query>          fails when the query returns nothing
 *     expect no rows: <query>       fails when it returns anything
 *     expect url /explorer          fails unless the url contains the fragment — this is what makes
 *                                   a nav step mean "it navigated", not "the label existed"
 *
 *   Escape hatches
 *     skip the Data view route: PopSQL runs in a cross-origin iframe
 *                                   recorded, never run, never moves the verdict
 *     [resolve: dropzone]           a control the docs cannot name (a dropzone, a hover-only icon)
 *
 * A plan never names a service or project id. `select the service` means whichever service the run
 * is about, and the id comes from the tool — an id written into a plan rots the next time a standing
 * service is recreated.
 */
---
